WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:00.140
Hey, folks.

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.100
My name is Sydney.

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:04.560
I'm the product manager for the open source team at LangChain.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.620
And today, I'm super excited to share with you

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.660
how you can build a model router in the harness

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.460
to reduce cost without sacrificing quality.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:13.940
Let's jump in.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.200
So the motivating problem for building a model router

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.340
is that frontier intelligence is really expensive.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.180
And so if you're building agents that work at scale,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.720
this cost can be prohibitive.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.100
And so model routing can help to reduce cost,

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.660
theoretically without sacrificing quality if it's done well.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.560
I have a quote here showing that model providers like

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.200
Anthropic even recommend starting with smaller models,

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.120
in this case like Claude Haiku, before graduating to a more

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.300
intelligent model like Opus, because a lot of tasks can be

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.480
done with these cheaper models.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.440
So I'm basically assuming here that we often don't need the

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.100
best model for a lot of tasks that we ask our agent to do.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.700
Past a certain point, you kind of hit diminishing returns

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.840
where your cost and latency are increasing,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.440
but the quality of the outputs are not significantly better.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.080
And ideally, an agent has good model-harness-task fit.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.020
And what does that mean?

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.060
It means you have the right model for the job

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.320
on the intelligence and cost curve here.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.760
And then your harness does a good job

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.940
of getting the right context to the model

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.300
for the given task at hand.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.840
So what does a model router actually do?

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.120
It picks the right model for each task.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.340
And so a task request comes in, and a router

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.560
has a classifier model, and then a prompt

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.840
with some criteria that help it decide what model would

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.540
be most appropriate for a given task.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.740
So would that be a fast and cheap model, maybe something

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.120
balanced, or a performance-focused model

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.380
that's going to be slower and more expensive?

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.260
And then it selects the given model for the task,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.800
and then that model is used to power the agent that

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:55.500
completes the task.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.800
And so our test bed here was Open SWE, our open source

00:01:59.020 --> 00:01:59.340
coding agent.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.080
And to kind of spoil the results of the experiment here,

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.200
we were able to see a 64% reduction in median cost

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.060
per thread with no measurable change in quality.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.120
There's four steps for building a model router,

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.380
and I'll explain them through the lens of this Open SWE

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.280
experiment that we ran.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.060
But a little sneak peek here.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.440
First, you need to understand the tasks

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:22.720
that your agent works on.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.000
You need to understand the models available for these tasks.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.420
Then with one and two done, you can build the router

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.760
in your agent's harness.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.960
And it's very important to track task outcomes

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.920
so that you can understand if your router is working well.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.580
That is reducing cost without sacrificing quality.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.000
OK, so let's talk about step one, understanding the tasks.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.540
The first thing that we did was try

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.400
to understand what our coding agent Open SWE is being

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.000
tasked with.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.240
This was all done using LangSmith.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.240
So every time our coding agent runs,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.700
it sends a trace to LangSmith that

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.780
has a record of every step that that agent took.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.260
And so we were able to use all of those traces

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.620
and do aggregate analysis over them

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.460
to determine what types of tasks our agent was doing.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.580
So you can see here, unsurprisingly, for a coding

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:15.120
agent that code changes dominated things like feature requests,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.120
bug fixes, and tests.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.500
But there were also some things like design questions

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.280
and general purpose questions.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.000
Out of all of the steps,

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.360
I would actually say that this one is the most important.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.780
This is what you use as the foundation

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.940
for building your criteria for the router.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.160
You know, deciding what types of tasks

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.320
should be assigned which model.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.640
And so if you don't have a clear understanding

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:43.600
of this task space,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.640
it's going to be very hard to develop an effective router.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.480
And LangSmith, as I mentioned, was very instrumental

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.700
in helping understand this space.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.340
I actually built a custom app on top of LangSmith

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.100
to help me better understand this trace data.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.300
So you can see kind of the categories here

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:04.500
and then active agent time and observed LLM cost

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.720
filtered by different types of requests.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.440
Overall, this was super helpful

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.000
for basically exploratory data analysis over my traces.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.000
Also as part of step one,

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.560
I wanted to better understand

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.040
how complexity varied by task type,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.080
knowing that we probably want a more intelligent model

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.780
for more complex tasks.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:28.560
So you can see here, we measured median invocations.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.260
So that's the number of times Open SWE was called.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.100
And then also the median LLM cost

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.880
for these different buckets of tasks.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:41.200
And then we generally rated a task as more complex

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.400
if the number of invocations were higher

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.580
or the LLM cost was higher.

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.060
And you can see kind of unsurprisingly here

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.700
that like feature work was generally more complex

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.700
than say testing work.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.000
All right, so this part is actually quite fun.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.460
Step two is understanding the models available

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.120
for your tasks.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.460
So this data is pulled from the

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.460
Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.800
as of September 9th, 2026,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.360
which is when we began this experiment.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.460
And you can see on the X-axis,

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.900
we have the cost per intelligence task.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.480
And then on the Y-axis,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.780
we have the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.940
This is what we call the Pareto frontier for LLMs.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.820
It's this set of optimal models that have a good trade-off

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.380
between like benchmark accuracy and evals

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:30.980
compared to cost and latency.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.680
And so generally we want to select models along this frontier.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.400
And then given that we were designing a model router,

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.240
we wanted to choose a small set of models here

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.620
that had some trade-offs between their speed

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:45.700
and intelligence index.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.280
The models that we ended up choosing

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.880
were GLM-5.3-Flash for our fast model, GPT-5.6

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.380
Sol for our balanced model, and then GPT-6 Astra

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:58.640
for our performance tier.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.380
Step three is building the router in the harness.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.560
So we used two sources of data to decide on the criteria

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.580
for our initial version of the router.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.380
The first was the understanding of those tasks

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:12.620
that we saw in step one.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.640
And the second was the official provider guides

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.320
from OpenAI, for example, on which models

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:20.480
to use for which tasks.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.640
And so you can see a couple of the descriptors

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.040
that we used for each of the criteria for a given model.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.700
Also important to note for our router here,

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.660
the router just runs once on the thread's first message

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.480
and then selects the model for the remainder of the task.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.640
This is different from some routers which

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:40.600
do this mid-flight routing.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.360
We decided to just do a simple version

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.600
first, but when I talk about upcoming improvements or further work, mid-thread routing is covered.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:54.840
So when we initially built this router in early September, we used a small LLM, specifically

00:06:55.180 --> 00:07:02.460
GLM-5.3-Flash, to do the routing decision with structured output. But after the experiment,

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:09.199
Jev, a popular decision model, came out and had claims of being significantly faster and cheaper

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.420
than LLMs with more constrained decision-making powers.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:17.000
And so we are now powering our Open SWE router with Jev,

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:17.780
the decision model.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.700
And it's much faster and much cheaper as Jev advertised.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:24.760
The last thing I'll call out on this slide

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:28.240
is that GLM-5.3-Flash is actually an open model.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.660
At LangChain a while ago, we wrote a blog

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.800
about open models crossing a threshold.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:34.660
We definitely think that's true.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.160
We use open models a lot internally.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.440
And a model router is a great opportunity

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.020
to experiment with using cheaper and faster open models

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:45.620
for tasks that they're more than equipped to handle.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:46.300
OK.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.620
And then for step four, you need to decide

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.560
how you're going to track your task outcomes.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:52.920
There's two main ways you can do this.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.200
The first is through offline evals.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:56.900
So this is very safe.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.380
You have repeatable comparisons and experiments

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:00.040
that you can run.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.460
But the catch is that it can be a little bit hard and expensive

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.340
to develop a representative data set.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:09.420
And so the faster approach here is to do an A/B test,

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:12.160
where you know that your live traffic is representative.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:15.580
But the catch here is that we're running this experiment

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.520
for our live users, and so they may experience

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:20.780
a non-optimal router, which is the risk.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:24.560
And so the two outcomes that we were tracking with our A/B

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.340
test were the merged PR rate.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.960
And then also, we enabled users to provide feedback

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.000
on Open SWE runs so they could either give a thumbs up

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:34.979
or a thumbs down.

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:38.020
And so you can imagine we want to make

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.960
sure that for the 50% of threads that

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.159
run through this new router, the merged PR rate

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:46.820
doesn't go down significantly, and the feedback

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.080
is not significantly different either.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.400
All right, so experiment number one, what were our results?

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.240
We had almost 500 threads.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.540
And you can see that the median cost per thread

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.540
for the router was much lower, 64% lower.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.780
The mean cost was also lower, and then the P90 cost per thread

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.080
was lower by 37%.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:11.380
We see actually for this case more threads

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.060
from the routed version ending in a merged PR,

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:17.160
although the difference was not statistically significant.

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.760
And then we also see that the PR open rate

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.080
was pretty comparable across our A and B branches.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:26.340
You can see on this breakdown kind of the proportion of tasks

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.060
that were sent to each of the model tiers.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.540
So we had 34% sent to the fast tier, 56% sent to the balanced

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.160
tier and only 10% sent to performance.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:39.540
You can see on the far left, kind of the different distribution

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:44.540
of costs for the always performance model versus the routed.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.060
And then you can see on the right side,

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:51.200
for the routed threads, we had much cheaper fast runs

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.420
and then the performance runs.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:56.780
And then on the right, you can see in aggregate,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.600
the fast runs were much cheaper and then balanced

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:02.220
and performance more expensive accordingly.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.980
One interesting note, we definitely

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.660
did see a couple of very long threads contributing

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.360
disproportionately to cost.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:13.080
And this was true for both the routed and non-routed arms

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:13.640
of the experiment.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.000
I will note, we actually started a second experiment,

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.480
which was router versus a controlled fast model.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.020
So in the first experiment, it was

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:23.920
a control of the performance model.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:26.340
We did end this test within a day

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.320
because we got so many complaints from users

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:30.160
who were stuck on the fast arm.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.000
And so we got enough complaints.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.600
we didn't want to significantly degrade user experience.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.940
And so we decided to stop this experiment.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:37.980
But it was pretty telling.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.860
We certainly couldn't switch over to GLM-5.3-Flash

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.140
for all of the tasks that Open SWE needs to manage.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:46.100
So what's next here?

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.140
There is definitely a lot that we could do to further explore

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:50.840
model routing optimizations.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:53.660
I think the first thing would be benchmarking the router

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.600
on other eval sets like Deep SWE,

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:58.600
rather than just doing the A/B testing,

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:02.080
or even perhaps other benchmarks that are more review focused,

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.260
because generally the task for Open SWE

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:07.540
is to generate good, reviewable PRs.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:09.640
The second thing that we didn't touch

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:11.740
is model selection for subagents.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:14.500
So there's definitely more cost optimizations to be made

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:17.340
if we also select appropriately sized models

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.800
for the subagents that the main agent orchestrates

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:21.980
with the Deep Agents harness.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:24.540
A third thing that I mentioned earlier

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:27.340
we could certainly explore is rerouting mid-thread.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:32.220
So you can imagine if for Open SWE, a user asks a question

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:35.640
and then discovers that maybe a pretty complex bug fix is

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:39.360
in order or wants to design a new feature mid-thread,

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:42.120
you might want to upgrade to a more capable model.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:44.940
And rerouting mid-thread could help

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:46.540
to ensure that the right model is

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:49.500
chosen for the task at any point in the given thread.

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:53.440
One trade-off here is that if you do switch a model,

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.260
you experience a cache miss, which can be costly.

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:58.640
But for async agents like Open SWE,

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.040
there's actually a lot of cache misses anyways,

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:04.420
just because there's more than a five minute idle time

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:06.240
between when an agent responds

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:09.220
and a developer has time to respond back in Slack.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.180
So this cache miss might actually not

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.380
be a super significant concern.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:16.380
And then the fourth thing would be

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.720
digging for richer routing signals.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:22.260
So we didn't get a ton of feedback on traces.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:24.520
We did get, you know, the PR merge rate

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:25.680
was a very reliable metric,

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:28.640
but trying to get more active feedback

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:29.580
would be helpful as well.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:31.560
All right, so if you want to get started

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:32.420
building your own router,

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.020
I would recommend following the four steps

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:36.220
that we did in this experiment.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:39.440
So one, understand your agent's task space.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:43.460
Two, pick models along the Pareto frontier,

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.120
the cost intelligence curve.

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:48.500
Third, treat routing as context engineering

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.860
and build your router into the harness

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:52.780
where the appropriate context is

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:54.580
for the given tasks at hand.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.160
And fourth, make sure you have the power to measure success

00:12:58.420 --> 00:12:59.880
before you begin live routing.

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:02.680
You can easily add routing to your own agent

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:04.220
with our model routing middleware.

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:07.400
And at LangChain, we've always prided ourselves

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:09.200
on offering open source software

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:11.440
